WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.050
SANDEEP GUPTA: Hi.

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:07.580
My name is Sandeep
Gupta, and I'm

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.340
a product manager in the
TensorFlow team in Google.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.920
Today, I will talk about
TensorFlow Enterprise

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.590
and how it can help you
in building and deploying

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:20.300
your ML workloads with
TensorFlow on GCP.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.230
Machine learning is transforming
every aspect of our lives,

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:25.880
and developers and
enterprises are

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.790
using ML to build
impactful solutions that

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.770
drive business value.

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:32.840
TensorFlow is one
of the most widely

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.790
used production-ready
frameworks for machine learning.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.460
And it's open-source
by Google so

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.670
that everyone can take advantage
of these powerful tools.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.750
But if you are an
enterprise trying to use ML,

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.500
there are some
challenges you may face.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.530
New TensorFlow open-source
releases come out several times

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:53.500
a year, and you may have
business reasons to stay

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.600
on an older version longer.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.080
In this case, you would want
that critical patches, fixes,

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:03.510
and enhancements be available
in these older versions.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.760
Second, Cloud
provides the ability

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.250
to build and deploy ML
with the performance

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.950
and scalability
that Cloud offers.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.320
This can be difficult,
as it may require

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.930
a lot of manual
configuration and setup

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.050
to make sure that you'll get the
best out of your infrastructure

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.490
investment.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:23.070
And lastly, you
expect prioritized

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:26.290
and trusted support.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:28.410
We designed
TensorFlow Enterprise

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.200
with the goals of addressing
these types of challenges

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.600
when it comes to
enterprise-grade ML.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.650
TensorFlow Enterprise is
the GCP-optimized experience

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.470
of TensorFlow.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.860
It consists of
three main pillars.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.640
First is performance
and the ability

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.860
to handle cloud-scale
data and models.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:51.550
TensorFlow Enterprise
includes optimizations

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.980
to help you get the most
from your data pipelines

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.770
and ML model training on GCP.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:59.530
Second is manageability.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:01.730
While solving your
business problem,

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.970
you do not want to spend
your time and energy

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:06.970
creating and maintaining
the software environment

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:10.330
and managing all the
machines in your cluster.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.790
You just want to focus
on the code business

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:16.700
logic of your ML problem that
matters to your business.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.360
TensorFlow Enterprise
makes it easier

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.950
to build and run ML workloads
with these managed services.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:28.090
And lastly, TFE releases
come with long-term support,

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:31.920
which provides backporting of
critical patches and fixes.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.850
So we'll look at all three
of these in more detail now.

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:39.160
Let's start by looking at
cloud-scale performance first.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:42.970
So at TensorFlow Enterprise,
we compile and ship a special

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:46.270
build of TensorFlow, which
is optimized for Google Cloud

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:47.710
infrastructure.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:50.140
It is built on
open-source TensorFlow,

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.870
but it contains add-ons
with additional runtime

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.540
optimizations and I/O
optimizations, which

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:58.900
are specific for Google
Cloud machines and services

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:00.980
to help improve performance.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.080
Let's take a look at an example.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.990
So this is a code
snippet that you

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:08.080
can use to train a model with,
potentially, a large training

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:11.170
data set in Google
Cloud Storage.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:13.980
Cloud storage is
GCP's file storage,

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.470
where you might maintain
terabytes of training data.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.600
As you see, this
code snippet is no

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.390
different from your typical
TensorFlow code written

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:26.740
with the tf.data API, except
that the path to the files

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:29.580
points to your GCS bucket.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.960
Under the hood,
TensorFlow Enterprise

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:34.260
build contains
optimized I/O readers

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.500
to make this performance
with Google Cloud Storage.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.900
Here's another example
where we read our training

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.550
data set from a BitQuery table.

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:46.320
BitQuery is GCP's
SQL-based data warehouse,

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.930
where you might maintain a
business intelligence data

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:52.950
link with hundreds of millions
of rows in many tables.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:56.470
And you might want to train
your models from all this data.

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:58.350
Here, we are
specifying the table

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.900
to read from, the
columns to read

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:04.440
and their data types, and
several other specifications

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.360
similar to the
standard tf.data API.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.970
And we are performing
additional transformations,

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:12.500
such as batching and shuffling.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.170
Under the hood, optimized
I/O for BitQuery

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:17.750
can read many millions
of rows in parallel

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.980
into the tensors efficiently.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:22.130
So with these
optimizations included,

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:25.280
you can get faster throughput
in your model training

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.410
with TensorFlow Enterprise.

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:29.720
Here is an example
on a recommendations

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.820
data set where we
see that we get

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:35.270
about a 30% higher throughput
with TensorFlow Enterprise

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:37.040
optimizations.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:40.040
This gain in data throughput
improves the utilization

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.890
of accelerators, such as
GPUs, because now, you

00:04:42.890 --> 00:04:47.350
are no longer bottlenecked by
I/O. It results in shorter wall

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:49.910
time of training for
the same accuracy,

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:52.460
as shown in the
chart on the left.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:56.350
The purple graph you see here
is the GPU utilization profile

00:04:56.350 --> 00:05:01.220
with TensorFlow Enterprise 1.15
on one model training example.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:03.620
You'll see that it
is narrower in time,

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.880
indicating shorter wall time,
and also attains a higher peak,

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:10.250
indicating higher
peak utilization.

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:13.610
Compared to the orange
graph, with TensorFlow 1.14,

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:16.710
which does not have
these optimizations.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.900
This better I/O throughput helps
reduce the cost of training.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:24.350
Also, with networking
optimizations to NCCL,

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.960
better performance is
achieved when you scale

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:29.600
to large clusters of GPUs.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.220
For example, when training
the BERT Large model

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:35.930
for NLP problems on
a 64 GPU cluster,

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:40.870
we see about a 30% speed up with
these improvements built in.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:42.670
So we have seen
some optimizations

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:45.520
for better scale and
performance on GCP

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.680
in TensorFlow Enterprise.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.320
Now, let's take a look at
how TensorFlow Enterprise

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.860
makes it easy to build ML
through managed services.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:58.650
We deliver TensorFlow Enterprise
through a managed environment.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:01.580
These are in the form of
deep learning VM images

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:03.650
and deep learning
container images,

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:05.960
where the environment
is pre-configured

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:10.340
on top of a standard Linux
box and made available to you.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:13.450
What makes this convenient
is that it pre-installs

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:16.070
the TensorFlow Enterprise
build, together

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:18.380
with all the
dependencies, including

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:21.590
device drivers, common
dependency packages,

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:25.670
and configurations to other
services on Google Cloud.

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:27.140
This means that
you no longer have

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:30.290
to think about installing all
the right packages and drivers

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:32.410
with correct
combination of versions

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:35.480
or wrangle with all of
their configurations.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:38.420
And support for all Google
Compute Engine security

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:41.090
features, such as
customer-managed encryption

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:45.530
keys and VPC, is built
in right out of the box.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:48.380
Because these are packaged
VM and container images,

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:51.260
you have many choices for
deploying them and using them

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:52.760
on the Cloud.

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:55.550
To get started, you pick
the TensorFlow Enterprise

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:59.270
image and the desired resources,
such as the machine type

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:03.110
and accelerator of choice, and
you start the virtual machine,

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:05.240
all with one line of command.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.610
Then, you can SSH
into the machine that

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:10.520
has TensorFlow Enterprise
installed with all dependencies

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:13.010
and device drivers
reconfigured, and you

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:15.230
can start using TensorFlow.

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:17.490
If you prefer a
notebook environment,

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:20.960
JupyterLab is hosted and
already started on the VM,

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.090
so you can go to the
browser and start

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:25.880
writing code in a notebook.

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:28.220
Once you have a
satisfactory model

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:30.560
after running some
experiments, now you

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:33.740
can train your models with the
full scale of your enterprise

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:35.120
data.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:37.190
It may not fit into
one machine and you

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:39.500
may want to take
advantage of distributed

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:43.220
training for large scale
of data and models.

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:46.860
GCP's AI Platform Training
is a managed service

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.200
for serverless
training that takes

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:52.440
care of distributed training
clusters and provisioning

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:54.690
of compute resources.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:58.350
More importantly, it drives
the same TensorFlow Enterprise

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:01.380
container, which has the
same environment as you have

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.020
developed the model with,
so you can be confident

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:08.160
that your model just trains
with the full scale of data

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.490
on AI Platform Training.

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:13.510
Here, we see an example
of how to do this.

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:16.980
You create a container, starting
with a TensorFlow Enterprise

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:18.270
base image.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:21.420
Overlay this with your
model training code.

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:24.600
Then, you issue one command
to start serverless training

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:26.380
on GCP.

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:29.350
In this example, we are using
10 workers for our distributed

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:34.841
training cluster, with eight
V100 GPUs attached to each.

00:08:34.841 --> 00:08:36.799
This brings up the
distributed training cluster

00:08:36.799 --> 00:08:39.890
with TensorFlow Enterprise
optimizations included.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:42.020
Very soon, Cloud AI
Platform Training

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:45.620
will use these DFE images
as the default runtime,

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:49.250
so you will not have to import
a DFE container manually,

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:51.070
as shown here.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:54.420
Now that you trained your
model in full enterprise scale,

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:57.460
it is time to make it
an end-to-end pipeline

00:08:57.460 --> 00:08:59.800
to continue running
in production.

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:02.400
This can be done using
GCP's AI Platform

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:07.570
Pipelines, which are built with
TensorFlow Extended, or TFX.

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:11.540
AI Platform Pipelines is hosted
on Google's Kubernetes Engine,

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:15.100
so it can also drive the same
TensorFlow Enterprise container

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:17.750
image, so that all the
optimizations are still

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:20.000
included, and you
can be confident

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:25.190
that your pipeline trains the
same way as your notebook code.

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:28.050
In addition to manage
training and hosted pipelines,

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:29.660
there are other
additions that make

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:32.690
TensorFlow run better on GCP
with TensorFlow Enterprise

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:34.250
add-ons.

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:36.200
Let me show you two examples.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:39.770
First, TensorFlow
Cloud repository's APIs

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:43.090
allow you to easily go
from debugging and training

00:09:43.090 --> 00:09:46.760
your Keras and TensorFlow
code in a local environment

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:49.280
to distributed
training in the cloud.

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:52.310
To use this, simply
add that the TF Cloud

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:56.550
run API call to your
Keras model training code.

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:59.430
It will take care of adding
the right TF distribution

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:04.090
strategy, based on the cluster
configuration that you provide.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:07.200
And a training job will
be triggered on GCP

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:10.620
using the cloud
AI platform APIs.

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:13.050
Hyperparameter tuning
is an important step

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:16.180
in fine tuning and
optimizing your model.

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:19.860
You can use the KerasTuner
API, conveniently within Keras,

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:23.370
to use hyperparameter tuning
in your model training.

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:25.830
CloudTuner is GCP's
implementation

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:27.630
of the KerasTuner API.

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:30.390
It is fully API-compatible
with KerasTuner

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:34.380
and uses GCP's optimizer
search algorithm under the hood

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:36.810
to run the
hyperparameter tuning.

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:39.000
These add-ons are
part of the standard

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.370
build of TensorFlow
enterprise and make

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:44.220
your journey from
offline experimentation

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:47.220
to cloud-scale
implementation easier.

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:49.680
Now that we have seen
cloud-scale performance

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:52.170
and cloud-integrated
managed services,

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:54.600
let's talk about
enterprise-grade support

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.270
that comes with
TensorFlow Enterprise.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:00.630
Key aspect of this
is long-term support.

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:03.750
Open-source TensorFlow comes
with a one-year-of-maintenance

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:04.860
window.

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.020
For TensorFlow
Enterprise, we extend that

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:09.810
and commit to providing
three years of support that

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:13.290
includes critical bug
fixes and security badges,

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:16.590
and optionally, backporting
important feature enhancements

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:20.800
from future releases, based
on customer needs and demand.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:22.620
This means that you
can rest assured

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:25.350
that you can upgrade
TensorFlow versions based

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:28.060
on your business's timelines.

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:32.110
As of today we support
both TensorFlow 1.x and 2.x

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:34.780
with TensorFlow Enterprise
long-term support.

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:37.330
Here's a great example of
what long-term support can

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:39.670
mean for you in practice.

00:11:39.670 --> 00:11:42.580
NVIDIA recently launched
their A100 family

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:46.840
of GPUs, which bring significant
performance improvements.

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.930
In collaboration with NVIDIA,
we have updated the TensorFlow

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:54.730
Enterprise 1.15 and 2.1
Deep Learning VM images

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:57.730
and added support
for CUDA 11, cuDNN8,

00:11:57.730 --> 00:12:00.790
and the new TensorFlow
32 data format

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:05.870
to help your code take advantage
of the A100 architecture.

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:07.600
These TensorFlow
Enterprise versions

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:11.770
are the only official builds
of TensorFlow 1.15 and 2.1

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:14.650
with this support built
in before this lands

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:17.240
in the mainline
open-source releases.

00:12:17.240 --> 00:12:19.570
You can become an early
user of these GPUs

00:12:19.570 --> 00:12:22.780
on GCP with these
pre-built images.

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:24.700
Getting started is really easy.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:26.620
With this example
gcloud command line

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:29.980
I've shown here, where we create
a TensorFlow Enterprise 2.1

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:34.330
VM which supports A100 GPUs.

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:37.570
You can learn more about these
GPUs in our cloud accelerator

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:38.890
session.

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:41.920
Before closing, let me
share what lies ahead

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:44.050
for TensorFlow Enterprise.

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:47.650
We are excited to keep working
tightly together between Google

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:50.050
Cloud and the TensorFlow teams.

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:52.870
As being the creators
and experts of both,

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:56.290
we plan to provide further
optimizations and enhancements

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:58.920
to TensorFlow on Google Cloud--

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:01.350
better monitoring and
debugging capabilities

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:05.260
to follow TensorFlow code and
integrations into Google Cloud

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:06.910
tooling.

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:08.920
We are also looking at
smoother integration

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:11.110
between TensorFlow's
high-level APIs,

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:14.050
such as Keras and the
training services,

00:13:14.050 --> 00:13:17.290
as well as more managed
services, such as Enterprise

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:21.430
version of tensorboard.dev
and integrated experiment

